    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/content-profiles.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Content types — `document` is the base, a paper extends it",
      "prov:value": "**No module lists the kinds.** They are DISCOVERED from the `block-kinds/`\ngraphs their owning harnesses declare — the document kinds in\nthe content core, the math kinds in the science layer (owner,\n2026-10-04: *\"kinds need to be discoverable … not centrally managed\"*; bean\nriit). Adding a kind is adding a node to the harness that owns it; the\n`BLOCK_KINDS`, `MATH_BLOCK_KINDS` and `DOCUMENT_BLOCK_KINDS` values in\n`schemas/content/block-kinds.ts` are computed from the nodes.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-04",
        "owner"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/content-profiles.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Two axes, and conflating them is the costly mistake",
      "prov:value": "**They pull in opposite directions, which is why the distinction is load-bearing\nrather than tidy.** Adapters must not overlap; profiles must. Making `document`\na third *adapter* would have made `adapterForKind` **ambiguous on every kind the\ntwo share** — eight of them, measured when the question was live — and a\nscoping function that returns two answers cannot fail an audit, because nothing\ncan say which answer was meant."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/content-profiles.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Two axes, and conflating them is the costly mistake",
      "prov:value": "**The instance that ships a content adapter declares it; the harness never\nnames it.** `contentAdapters` in that instance's `<instance>.json` —\n`contentType`, instance-relative `module`, `className`, and `extends` for the\ntype it specialises — is what `src/builtin-adapters.ts` discovers. `extends`\norders the fallback: the specialisation leads, because falling back *to* a\nsuperset loses nothing and falling back *from* it loses its tools. The harness\nheld `../<instance>/adapters/…` paths until 2026-09-30, a variable import no\nstatic gate could see and the edge that kept it from lifting into its own\nrepository; `check:import-direction` now fails on that shape (bean `p11x`).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean p11x",
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/content-profiles.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A normative statement is not a block kind",
      "prov:value": "**Known wrong, and left visible rather than quietly patched:** intake guidance\nthat maps guideline recommendations onto `definition` predates the document\nprofile and is wrong for a document folio, where `definition`'s formal field is\nrequired."
    }
  ]
}
